---
title: Tomato salsa
description: Lesson 7.1. Holding the knife, guarding your fingers, and dicing an onion.
servings: 4
time: 25 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, mastering-the-knife]
lesson: "7.1"
technique: grip and dicing
status: draft
---

> Technique: the grip, the guiding hand and the dice. Two hands do the
> work. One holds the knife by pinching the blade; the other holds the
> food in a claw and sets the width of every cut. Go slowly: accuracy
> first, and speed arrives on its own.

Set a #chopping board{} on a damp cloth so it cannot slide. Hold a #chef's knife{} by pinching the blade where it meets the handle between your thumb and forefinger, with the other three fingers round the handle.

> Why pinch the blade: it lets you choke up on the knife and gives you
> control and leverage over its whole length. Holding only the handle
> gives much less control over the tip.

Curl the fingers of your other hand into a claw on top of the food, fingertips tucked back, so that the flat of the blade slides against your knuckles.

> Why the claw: your knuckles guide the side of the blade and your
> fingertips stay out of its way. After each cut the claw steps back
> by the width of the next slice.
-- source: Liz Bomze, "A Guide to Using Your Chef's Knife", America's Test Kitchen, 2018-12-05, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/1362-a-guide-to-using-your-chef-s-knife

Cut @onion{1/2}(small) in half from top to root, through the root, and peel it. Lay a half flat side down. Make three or four horizontal cuts towards the root without cutting through it, then cuts down from top to root about 5 mm apart, again leaving the root whole. Now slice across those cuts and the onion falls into dice.

> Why the root stays on: it holds the layers together while you make
> every cut.
-- source: "How to Cut an Onion", America's Test Kitchen, 2016-04-12, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/229-how-to-cut-an-onion

> Science: cutting an onion breaks its cells, and an enzyme reaction
> then turns its sulfur compounds into propanethial S-oxide, the
> vapour that makes eyes water. A 2025 physics preprint filmed the
> juice spraying out as a blade breaks the onion's tight outer skin:
> blunter blades and faster cuts threw more droplets with more
> energy, the count rising up to forty-fold between the sharpest and
> bluntest blades tested. The
> rig used a steel blade, not a kitchen knife, and counted droplets,
> not tears. Look up: lachrymatory factor, propanethial S-oxide.
-- source: Wu, Hooshanginejad, Wang, Hui and Jung, "Droplet Outbursts from Onion Cutting", preprint arXiv:2505.06016, 2025, https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.06016 (a steel blade in a test rig, not a kitchen knife; it measures droplets, not tears)

Cut @tomatoes{400%g}(ripe) into quarters, then into 1 cm dice. Halve @green chilli{1}, scrape out the seeds and chop it finely. Chop @coriander{15%g}(leaves and thin stalks).

> Why a sharp knife: a dull blade needs more force and is more likely
> to slip. You can hear it: a dull knife crunches through an onion and
> a sharp one glides.
-- source: Liz Bomze, "A Guide to Using Your Chef's Knife", America's Test Kitchen, 2018-12-05, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/1362-a-guide-to-using-your-chef-s-knife ; Miye Bromberg, "How Often Should You Sharpen Your Knife? Use an Onion to Find Out.", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-05-16, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/5519-how-often-should-you-sharpen-your-knife-just-use-your-onion

Mix everything in a #bowl{} with the juice of @lime{1} and @salt{1/2%tsp}. Leave for ~{10%minutes}, then taste and adjust the salt and lime.

> Next time, without the recipe: pinch the blade, claw the food, and
> give anything round a flat side to sit on before you cut it. For an
> onion: halve through the root, cut towards the root twice, then
> across.
